WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.062 --> 00:00:12.378
Raoul sighed impatiently as he sat next to the Persian on the floor of the third cellar. "I'll listen to your

2
00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:21.149
story but only so you'll lead me to Christine." The Persian nodded and began his tale. "I'd frequently asked Erik

3
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:28.132
to show me his house on the lake, but he refused. Although I spied on him from the opposite shore, it

4
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:33.751
was always too dark to see the entrance and how he opened it. One day, thinking I was alone, I

5
00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:45.532
stepped into the boat and began to row. The house itself was invisible from the lake because it was hidden behind

6
00:00:45.533 --> 00:00:50.361
the cellar wall. Immediately the silence was broken by soft singing that rose from the waters and surrounded me." Raoul's

7
00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:59.235
eyes widened in fear. "The siren!" The Persian nodded. "The voice followed me as I rowed, but it was so lovely

8
00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:09.597
that I wasn't alarmed. On the contrary, I wanted to discover the source of that sweet and enticing music. I

9
00:01:09.598 --> 00:01:18.119
leaned out of the boat and over the water—" "Oh, no!" Raoul's hurry to find Christine was temporarily forgotten as he

10
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:26.058
got caught up in the Persian's story. "Although I was in the middle of the lake," the Persian continued, "I

11
00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:35.373
felt perfectly safe because the water was calm. I had no doubt that I was face-to-face with some new trick of

12
00:01:35.374 --> 00:01:41.738
Erik's. But this trick was so perfect that I didn't care how he'd created it. I only wanted to get closer

13
00:01:41.739 --> 00:01:45.185
to the music, so I leaned and leaned—" "And the boat tipped over?" Raoul asked. The Persian shook his head.

14
00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:52.914
"Suddenly two huge arms reached up and seized me by the neck. I was dragged underwater and would have drowned if

15
00:01:52.915 --> 00:02:01.861
I hadn't fought back. Gasping for air, I reached the surface and cried out. Fortunately, Erik recognized my voice, and

16
00:02:01.862 --> 00:02:09.918
instead of drowning me, he rescued me and laid me on the edge of the lake." "Was he upset that you

17
00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:13.113
were trying to find his house?" "Yes, he was very angry. He said, 'I never invited you! I don't want

18
00:02:13.114 --> 00:02:24.506
you here!' I tried to pacify him by asking how he'd created the siren. He laughed and showed me a long

19
00:02:24.507 --> 00:02:30.477
reed, which he used for breathing and singing underwater." "It sounds like he was proud of his trick," Raoul said.

20
00:02:30.478 --> 00:02:34.711
"Oh, he was. In some respects Erik is like a child who craves attention, but I scolded him severely. His trick

21
00:02:35.982 --> 00:02:44.398
had almost killed me, and I had no doubt that it had already been fatal to others. I reminded him that

22
00:02:44.399 --> 00:02:54.471
he'd promised me no more murders. 'Have I really committed murders?' he asked in a cheerful voice, trying to disarm

23
00:02:54.472 --> 00:03:00.459
me." "And had he?" Raoul asked. "Oh, yes, back when we both lived in Persia." "Christine is with a murderer!" Raoul

24
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.910
leaped up. "We must rescue her at once!" "In a few minutes." The Persian motioned for Raoul to sit down.

25
00:03:06.911 --> 00:03:13.306
"Erik said he'd rather forget the past, but I reminded him that I'd saved his life years ago. I wanted him

26
00:03:13.307 --> 00:03:26.171
to promise again that there would be no more murders, but he refused. 'As you well know,' he said, 'I

27
00:03:27.112 --> 00:03:37.602
never keep my promises.'" Raoul was shocked by what he was hearing. "A woman died when the chandelier fell during the

28
00:03:37.603 --> 00:03:47.009
opera. Was that another of Erik's fatal tricks?" "I asked him about that," the Persian said. "He denied any involvement,

29
00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:56.071
saying that the chandelier was old and worn and fell on its own. But he laughed so horribly that I wasn't

30
00:03:56.072 --> 00:04:04.736
sure what to believe. He warned me not to try to enter his house again. Then he said he'd hate

31
00:04:04.737 --> 00:04:11.329
to have to dedicate his funeral mass to me, which I took as a death threat." "So, that's why we're not

32
00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:16.569
entering the house from the lake," Raoul said. The Persian nodded. "That entrance is obviously well-guarded, especially since Erik realized I

33
00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:25.090
knew about it. But I figured there was another entrance because I'd often seen him disappear in the third cellar."

34
00:04:25.091 --> 00:04:31.062
"And that's where we are right now?" "Exactly. Ever since I discovered Erik living at the opera house, I've feared that

35
00:04:31.063 --> 00:04:41.720
he might hurt someone. Whenever some accident happened, everyone blamed the ghost, but I suspected Erik," the Persian said. "Although

36
00:04:41.721 --> 00:04:49.787
people often laughed about the opera ghost, I wondered how they'd react if they knew the phantom was a real person."

37
00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:57.638
"How did you learn about the revolving mirror in Christine's dressing room?" "Erik told me that he was a changed

38
00:04:57.639 --> 00:05:07.788
person since he was loved for himself," the Persian said. "I didn't know what he meant, but I suspected that Christine

39
00:05:07.789 --> 00:05:13.740
might be involved. So I hid in the storage room next to her dressing room and listened while he gave

40
00:05:14.727 --> 00:05:28.183
her singing lessons. His voice could be as loud as thunder or as soft as an angel's, and it was always

41
00:05:28.184 --> 00:05:37.848
beautiful. But I couldn't understand how his voice, no matter how marvelous, could make her forget his frightening face. And then

42
00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:43.568
it dawned on me—" "Christine had not yet seen him!" Raoul finished the Persian's sentence. "Exactly. Later on I found

43
00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:50.691
the trapdoor that enabled Erik to go straight to the cellars from the stage. I also went into Christine's dressing room

44
00:05:50.692 --> 00:05:59.661
and discovered the hidden button that moved the mirror. Behind the mirror, I found the passage leading to the spring."

45
00:05:59.662 --> 00:06:06.740
"Christine described to me how he took her through that passage and put her on a white horse," Raoul said. "So

46
00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:13.893
you won't be surprised when I tell you I witnessed him bathing her temples with water at the spring. The

47
00:06:13.894 --> 00:06:19.718
white horse, which I'm sure he'd stolen from the opera stables, waited patiently nearby. When I revealed myself, it was terrible.

48
00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:29.613
Sparks flew from Erik's eyes, but before I could speak, I received a blow to the head that stunned me."

49
00:06:29.614 --> 00:06:37.548
"You mean he knocked you out?" "Yes, and when I came to, Erik, Christine, and the horse were gone. I felt

50
00:06:37.549 --> 00:06:43.251
sure that Erik had taken that poor girl prisoner and was keeping her in his house. Despite Erik's warnings and

51
00:06:43.252 --> 00:06:54.425
threats, I was determined to return to the edge of the lake. I hid there overnight, waiting for him to reappear."

